---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";

const title = "Connect iCloud Calendar to Pikos";
const description = "Sync an iCloud calendar into Pikos over CalDAV. Make an app-specific password, connect, and your events arrive as pages you can take notes on. Read-only: nothing goes back to Apple.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-8 py-20">

    <a href="/calendar-sync" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-neutral-400 transition-colors hover:text-primary-500 dark:text-neutral-500 dark:hover:text-primary-400">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg>
      Calendar sync
    </a>

    <article>
      <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Connect iCloud Calendar</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500">Three steps. You'll need two-factor authentication turned on for your Apple Account.</p>

      <Prose class="mt-10">

        <p>
          Sync is read-only and opt-in. Your events come into Pikos as pages you can write on, and nothing you do in Pikos is ever sent back to Apple. Your calendar cannot be changed or damaged by connecting it here.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Make an app-specific password</h2>

        <p>
          Sign in at <a href="https://account.apple.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">account.apple.com</a>, go to <strong>Sign-In and Security</strong>, then <strong>App-Specific Passwords</strong>. Create one, name it "Pikos", and copy what it gives you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Don't use your normal Apple password. iCloud won't accept it for CalDAV, and an app password is one you can delete later to cut Pikos off without touching anything else.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Connect in Pikos</h2>

        <p>
          Open Settings with <code>Cmd+,</code>, go to the <strong>Calendar Sync</strong> tab, click <strong>Add account</strong> and choose <strong>CalDAV</strong>. Fill in:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Server URL</strong> &mdash; <code>https://caldav.icloud.com</code></li>
          <li><strong>Username</strong> &mdash; your Apple Account email</li>
          <li><strong>Password</strong> &mdash; the app-specific password from step 1</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          You don't need to find individual calendar addresses. Pikos discovers them from the base URL, including the partition host Apple redirects you to.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Pick the calendars you want</h2>

        <p>
          Pikos lists the calendars on the account that hold events. Turn on the ones you want and leave the rest alone. Each one gets its own folder and a colour you can change.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your app password goes straight into the macOS keychain, not into a file Pikos owns.
        </p>

        <h2>What to expect afterwards</h2>

        <p>
          Pikos brings in the past week and everything ahead of it. Older events stay in iCloud and don't come across, so a freshly connected account looks thinner than the calendar you're used to.
        </p>

        <p>
          The title, time and recurrence of a synced event are locked, because that's how it stays true to the source. The notes you add to it are yours outright and sync never overwrites them. Turning a calendar off later removes the mirrored events and keeps anything you wrote.
        </p>

        <h2>If it doesn't connect</h2>

        <p>
          The usual cause is the account password rather than an app-specific one. The second is two-factor authentication not being on, which Apple requires before it will issue app passwords at all.
        </p>

      </Prose>

      <div class="mt-16 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50 px-6 py-6 dark:border-neutral-800 dark:bg-neutral-900">
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">
          How synced events behave in full, plus Google and other providers, is on the <a href="/calendar-sync" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">calendar sync page</a>. Don't have Pikos yet? It's a <a href="/download" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">free download</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

    </article>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
